Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["RC Low-Pass Filter from Brian", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9772897343047647`*^9, 3.977289742778789*^9}, {
  3.977557185041368*^9, 3.977557199818494*^9}, {3.977560920391399*^9, 
  3.9775609285410013`*^9}, {3.977561704893649*^9, 3.9775617124536877`*^9}, {
  3.977562699357638*^9, 3.977562712075577*^9}, {3.977935734236527*^9, 
  3.977935734945239*^9}, {3.98414118498368*^9, 3.984141192106823*^9}, {
  3.9841413327414618`*^9, 3.984141401545951*^9}, {3.9841426994209747`*^9, 
  3.9841427009614687`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f5f1e433-226c-480d-b80d-\
61a4d380bb8f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["f", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "0ff10ee1-5f6d-43fe-a1fe-58975ca48682"],
 " is the frequency of a signal, usually specified in Hz, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["C", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "9f40a842-04e9-492d-99c6-d9bee9bfcd61"],
 " is a capacitance, usually measured in microfarads, nanofarads, or \
picofarads, then there is a strange sense in which ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   FractionBox["1", 
    RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]", " ", "f", " ", "C"}]], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "ccca305b-0223-457a-8c23-9873f02ad522"],
 " can be thought of as a resistance. A capacitor is not really a resistor, \
but go along with it. At least this combination has the units of Ohms, like a \
resistor! On the next page are some problems that build on this weird idea."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.984142733498555*^9, 3.9841429774624233`*^9}, {
  3.984143036266165*^9, 3.984143072896241*^9}, {3.984143106516336*^9, 
  3.984143205128378*^9}, {3.9841469431782637`*^9, 3.9841469879801607`*^9}, {
  3.984147200937603*^9, 3.984147201612522*^9}, {3.984151462421432*^9, 
  3.9841514952229633`*^9}, {3.984151554223268*^9, 3.984151560195731*^9}, {
  3.9841516251371098`*^9, 3.9841517265055237`*^9}, {3.9841533985760307`*^9, 
  3.984153414610731*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1ff75892-182f-438b-ba88-\
18c673cf39c3"],

Cell["", "PageBreak",
 PageBreakBelow->True,ExpressionUUID->"fc908096-420d-49f1-bdc8-11f24c86f018"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
7. A Strange Way of Thinking about Capacitors \[LongDash] High Frequencies \
Pass Easier Through a Capacitor than Low Frequencies\
\>", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9841413107635937`*^9, 3.9841413110641823`*^9}, {
  3.984141352946575*^9, 3.984141377413493*^9}, {3.984141409068183*^9, 
  3.984141409288742*^9}, {3.9841427153633413`*^9, 3.9841427305400553`*^9}, {
  3.984151512490959*^9, 
  3.9841515378817673`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c78a31fd-7d4b-4136-976b-\
0eff5ba1129f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(a) If a capacitor has capacitance ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"C", "=", 
    RowBox[{"100", " ", "nF"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "a760fb7c-ff3d-408f-a72a-96380e74ef78"],
 " (nanofarads) and a low-frequency signal with frequency ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", "=", 
    RowBox[{"50", "Hz"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "62c2ff3f-f397-4f0f-b20a-2be6c83017b3"],
 " is applied, what is the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]not really a resistance\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] resistance of the capacitor? Call your answer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"R", "'"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "0497c325-10ee-40a8-8a1e-0e7cdfdbd160"],
 " and round to the nearest significant figure. You will use this result in \
8(a).\n\n(b) If the same capacitor has an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", "=", 
    RowBox[{"5000", "Hz", " "}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "c2ef03f0-6a9f-43e7-b95e-2e26c8a8dc31"],
 "signal applied to it, what is the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]not really a \
resistance\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] resistance? Call that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"R", "''"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "bf94c11d-e710-4549-9e54-8c48e60742c3"],
 " and again round to the nearest significant figure. You will use this \
result in 8(b).\n\nCROSSCHECK: Your answer for (b) should be 100x smaller \
than your answer for (a). Notice that a capacitor\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]not really a resistance\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
resistance decreases as frequency increases."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.984142733498555*^9, 3.9841429774624233`*^9}, {
  3.984143036266165*^9, 3.984143072896241*^9}, {3.984143106516336*^9, 
